This research addresses the problem of restoring natural monotonicity properties in the solutions of heuristic or approximation algorithms, which often fail to satisfy them. It proposes a black-box meta-algorithm, Mf, that, given oracle access to a possibly non-monotone multi-dimensional real-valued function f, effectively restores monotonicity. The algorithm guarantees feasibility (Mf(x) <= max(y<=x) f(y)) and maintains an expected solution quality comparable to the original function (E[Mf(x)] >= E[f(x)] - 
). For general monotonicity, the algorithm achieves a query complexity of O(log(d/
))^d, with logarithmic dependence on 1/
 but exponential dependence on the number of parameters d. A key contribution is the theoretical proof that this exponential dependence on d is fundamentally necessary for black-box monotonicity correction. Furthermore, the paper introduces and develops an improved meta-algorithm for the weaker property of k-marginal monotonicity, where query complexity scales exponentially with k (O((d/
)^k)), offering a significant improvement for small k compared to full monotonicity.
